The Sinkhorn algorithm remains a widely-used method for computing the optimal transport metric between two probability distributions. Despite its reliance on gradient descent, the algorithm exhibits convergence even when its classical formulation is unfeasible. This paper demonstrates that a modified Sinkhorn algorithm can effectively converge and reconstruct the optimal transport plan, even in complex cases. To achieve this, the algorithm incorporates a form of regularization that mitigates the singularities present in certain problems. Our research suggests that a sufficient amount of regularization can be achieved through the method's inherent parameters. To support our findings, we include simple numerical examples to illustrate the algorithm's performance and efficacy. [Our contributions can be summarized as follows: (1) Introducing a modified Sinkhorn algorithm that accounts for the presence of singularities (2) Proving the algorithm's convergence to a reconstructed solution (3) Discussing the implications of the modified algorithm for its implementation in practice] [For the full version of this article, including supplementary material, visit: https://sites.google.com/view/mathematique/ home/about/optimisation] [/abstract]

